 Key Changes and How to Use EmailJS: 

 1. 

 -----

## Button Text Updates

All instances of "Get Started" and "Try Now" have been replaced with **"Schedule Demo"**.

 2. 

 -----

## Modal Integration

 * A Bootstrap modal with the ID `demoModal` has been added to the end of the `<body>` before the scripts.
 * This modal includes a form with fields for **Full Name**, **Email Address**, and **Message**.
 * The modal's styling (`modal-content`, `modal-header`, `modal-body`, `form-control`, etc.) has been carefully adjusted in the `<style>` section to match your existing dark theme and color palette (using `var(--secondary)`, `var(--neutral)`, `var(--primary)`, and `var(--accent)`).
 * The "Schedule Demo" buttons now include `data-bs-toggle="modal"` and `data-bs-target="#demoModal"` attributes to trigger the modal.

 3. 

 -----

## EmailJS Setup

To make the form functional with EmailJS, you need to replace the placeholder values in the script section:

 * **`YOUR_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY`**: You'll find this in your EmailJS account under **Account** -\> **API Keys**.
 * **`YOUR_SERVICE_ID`**: This is the ID of the Email Service you've set up in EmailJS (e.g., `service_xxxxxx`).
 * **`YOUR_TEMPLATE_ID`**: This is the ID of the Email Template you've created in EmailJS (e.g., `template_xxxxxx`).

**Important:** Go to the EmailJS website, set up your service (e.g., Gmail), and create an email template. The form fields (`name="from_name"`, `name="from_email"`, `name="message"`) in the HTML form directly correspond to the variables you'll use in your EmailJS template (e.g., `{{from_name}}`, `{{from_email}}`, `{{message}}`).

```javascript
emailjs.init({
 publicKey: "YOUR_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY", // <--- IMPORTANT: Replace with your actual EmailJS Public Key
});

// ... inside the event listener for demoForm submission
emailjs.sendForm('YOUR_SERVICE_ID', 'YOUR_TEMPLATE_ID', this) // <--- IMPORTANT: Replace with your actual EmailJS Service ID and Template ID
```

I've also added a simple success/failure message (`formMessage`) that will appear below the form after submission, and a basic loading state for the button.

 Now, when a user clicks on "Schedule Demo," the modal will pop up, they can fill out the form, and upon submission, the details will be sent to your inbox via EmailJS. 

