 To fix this and allow you to pass branch names directly, we need to add some logic to the top of the Makefile to parse those arguments and assign them to your MCP_BRANCH and AGENTIC_BRANCH variables. 

 This new version will be flexible and support three ways of running: 

 New Way: make dev <mcp_branch> <agentic_branch> 

 Old Way: make dev MCP_BRANCH=<mcp_branch> AGENTIC_BRANCH=<agentic_branch> 

 Default Way: make dev (uses the default branches) 

 The Solution: Argument Parsing Logic 

 We will add a few lines at the top of your Makefile . This code captures any command-line arguments after dev , assigns them to the branch variables, and sets defaults if no arguments are provided. 

 # --- Start of New Argument Parsing Logic ---

# Get all arguments passed to make, and filter out the 'dev' target itself.
# This leaves us with the branch names provided as arguments.
BRANCH_ARGS := $(filter-out dev,$(MAKECMDGOALS))

# Extract the first and second branch names from the arguments.
MCP_BRANCH_FROM_ARGS := $(word 1, $(BRANCH_ARGS))
AGENTIC_BRANCH_FROM_ARGS := $(word 2, $(BRANCH_ARGS))

# Define the final branch variables with a clear priority:
# 1. Use the positional argument if provided (e.g., make dev branch1).
# 2. Else, use the variable assignment if provided (e.g., make dev MCP_BRANCH=branch1).
# 3. Else, use the hardcoded default.
MCP_BRANCH := $(or $(MCP_BRANCH_FROM_ARGS),$(MCP_BRANCH),mcp3.0)
AGENTIC_BRANCH := $(or $(AGENTIC_BRANCH_FROM_ARGS),$(AGENTIC_BRANCH),feature/workflow_multiframework-a2a)

# --- End of New Argument Parsing Logic ---

